\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(R1): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Validation on two real-world clinical datasets, the METABRIC breast cancer cohort \citep {curtis2012genomic} and the SUPPORT study of seriously ill hospitalized adults \citep {knaus1995support}, confirms the calibration-discrimination decoupling: IBS saturates at a constant value as network width grows while concordance varies, reproducing the primary synthetic finding across distinct clinical domains and sample sizes.}}{2}{section*.1}%
\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(R1): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{where $f^*$ denotes the true data-generating function and $\hat {f}_{\bm {\theta }}$ denotes the model's estimate parameterized by ${\bm {\theta }}$.}}{5}{section*.2}%
\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(R1): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Understanding double descent is important for both theoretical and practical reasons. At the interpolation threshold, the unique interpolating solution must fit training noise exactly, producing maximum variance among all models in the capacity landscape. The information matrix becomes singular at $P = N$, and the resulting sensitivity to training set perturbations yields worst-case generalization. In clinical survival modeling, this has direct consequences: a practitioner conducting hyperparameter search across network widths may unknowingly select a model in the threshold region, basing treatment decisions, resource allocation, or patient counseling on the worst-performing configuration. Standard model selection by concordance alone would not detect the accompanying calibration failure (Section\nobreakspace {}\ref {sec:mechanism}), potentially producing overconfident risk estimates. Although $\ell _2$ regularization is included by default in DeepSurv implementations \citep {katzman2018deepsurv}, our experiments show that it attenuates but does not eliminate the concordance dip and, critically, does not address calibration failure (Section\nobreakspace {}4.5).}}{6}{section*.3}%
\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(R1): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ \citep {uno2011c, hartman2023pitfalls}}}{7}{section*.4}%
\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(R1): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{, and is sensitive to extreme risk scores in a way that the concordance index is not \citep {austin2020graphical}}}{7}{section*.5}%
\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(R1): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{for the primary analysis and validate key findings on real-world clinical data (Section\nobreakspace {}\ref {sec:realdata})}}{7}{section*.6}%
\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(R1): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The baseline scenarios (A--D) use a linear log-hazard as a deliberate control: if double descent occurs even when the true model is correctly specified as a linear Cox model, the phenomenon must arise from the optimization landscape of overparameterized neural networks rather than from model-data mismatch. Scenario\nobreakspace {}E introduces nonlinear ground truth (interaction and quadratic terms) to test the converse.}}{8}{section*.7}%
\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(R1): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{eight}}{8}{section*.8}%
